Fillets: a rounding whose contact leaves its face N across a convex edge E
into the face B at the end, short of the spine's end, where E holds the end
vertex V, rolls on E, and B cuts the blend from where the ball's contact on
E turns back, where the walk over N's surface past E does not close on B
(mitcad#1389).

A bar's side is rounded with 0.2 along its chain, which ends at a vertex V
of a narrow lip N (a plane 0.34 wide), the rounding R of the bar's
underside (a cylinder of 0.5) and a notch B for a tube (a concave cylinder
of 13.5). N's edge E with B (a line holding V) is convex, 29.5 degrees.
Coming along the chain, the rounding's contact on N reaches E before the
section at V. ChFi3d_ObstacleBeforeEnd (0620) leaves edges at the spine's
end vertex and faces beyond holding it to the end's handling, so the walk
went on over N's surface past E; its contact on R ran on onto the
underside (R goes on tangentially into it) before reaching R's edge Eq with
B, and the corner cut that blend by B: two faces at each corner, the
underside cut, 0.015 mm off the stored bodies. The stored bodies roll the
ball on E, touching R: its contact on E runs towards V and turns back at a
point T, and B cuts the rest of the rolling blend from T down to where its
contact on R reaches Eq, the underside untouched; three faces (the rounding
up to E, the blend rolling on E up to T's section, the cut piece ending in
a point on Eq). Where the walk past E does close on B (its other contact
reaches Eq within its face), the stored bodies keep the blend over N's
surface cut by B, as before: the same bar's chain at its other end.

- PerformSetOfSurf: where an end was walked on past such an edge and its
  other contact ran onto another face short of Eq
  (ChFi3d_WalkedOnPastEndEdge), the stripe is walked again with that end in
  myRollEndEdgeSpines: there ComputeData takes the contact on E for an
  obstacle (ChFi3d_RollsOnEndEdge: a convex edge holding the end vertex
  into a face at the end, more than 10 tolesp before the end) and StartSol
  takes E for the edge the next piece rolls on
  (ChFi3d_ConvexEdgeIntoEndFace). The piece rolling on E is then split at
  the point T where its contact on E comes nearest V, the part past T cut
  by B from T on E to the point Q where its other contact reaches Eq
  (ChFi3d_NarrowCutPiece, on a surface of its own), the part before T
  rolling on E from T (ChFi3d_TrimWalkedPiece), and the stripe ends in Q
  with no corner at V (ChFi3d_RolledOnEndEdgeCutAtTurn). Where any of that
  fails, the stripe is walked again as before.

On the design's body the rounding (22 edges, both sides) gives the stored
body's three faces at each corner, valid, the result and the stored body
within 0.0011 mm of each other about the corners (0.018 mm before); with
MitCad's dressup the item gives the stored state's 223 faces (221 before).

Test: geometry/tests/test_fillet_end_edge_turn.cpp, on the case
`notch-lip-corners` of MITCAD_GEOMETRY_CASES.

Upstream: new.

